clautolisp: the vlr-* reactor builtins call two functions that are
undefined in their own package, so they signal at runtime.

Found 2026-08-01 while fixing the same bug class in alfe
(alfe-bricscad-automation-macos): a from-scratch rebuild reports

  ; caught WARNING:
  ;   undefined function: CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-BUILTINS-CORE::REACTOR-TYPE-NAME
  ;   undefined function: CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-BUILTINS-CORE::DOCUMENT-REACTOR-REGISTRY


* Cause

Both functions are defined in clautolisp.autolisp-runtime
(autolisp-runtime/source/ontology.lisp:86 and :102) and exported from
its package (autolisp-runtime/source/package.lisp:280, :282).

clautolisp.autolisp-builtins-core does NOT :USE that package — it
imports what it needs one name at a time — and its long reactor
:IMPORT-FROM block (autolisp-builtins-core/source/package.lisp:80-102)
lists reactor-scope, reactor-kind, reactor-type-scope,
reactor-type-keywords, add-reactor-to-document, ... but NOT
reactor-type-name and NOT document-reactor-registry.

So those two names read as FRESH INTERNAL symbols of
clautolisp.autolisp-builtins-core. At compile time that is only a
style warning; at run time the call is a fatal undefined-function
error.

Proof (load the clautolisp system, then):

  REACTOR-TYPE-NAME:         package=CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-BUILTINS-CORE status=INTERNAL fboundp=NIL
  DOCUMENT-REACTOR-REGISTRY: package=CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-BUILTINS-CORE status=INTERNAL fboundp=NIL
  REACTOR-SCOPE:             package=CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-RUNTIME       status=INTERNAL fboundp=#<FUNCTION ...>

REACTOR-SCOPE is the control: a correctly imported sibling resolves to
the runtime's bound function.


* Reachable from user code

  autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp:5971,5975 — INSTALL-NEW-REACTOR

      (current-document-or-error (reactor-type-name kind))

    The argument is evaluated eagerly, so this is not an error-only
    path: EVERY vlr-*-reactor constructor generated by
    DEFINE-VLR-CONSTRUCTOR goes through it — (vlr-object-reactor ...),
    (vlr-acdb-reactor ...), (vlr-editor-reactor ...), and so on.

  autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp:6202 — BUILTIN-VLR-PERS-LIST

      (gethash id (document-reactor-registry document))

    Hit by (vlr-pers-list) as soon as the persistent-reactor index has
    an entry.

That the test corpus is green means no test constructs a reactor or
calls vlr-pers-list — worth a look in its own right.


* Fix

Add the two names to the reactor :IMPORT-FROM block in
clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/package.lisp. Then add
regression coverage: a test that constructs a reactor via a vlr-*
constructor and one that calls vlr-pers-list, plus — to catch the whole
class — a check that each name the builtins call resolves to a bound
function in its home package (alfe now has such a test:
bricscad-package-imports-the-launcher-liveness-helpers).

Bump the clautolisp version per convention.


* Note on the class of bug

A package that :USEs only CL and imports name-by-name turns a missing
import into a runtime undefined-function error, while the compiler only
whispers a style warning. The build prints it, but it scrolls past in a
green run. Worth a CI check that fails the build on any
"undefined function" in a from-scratch compile — the same slip shipped
in alfe on 2026-08-01 and was caught only by a real CAD run.


* Resolution (1.7.7, 2026-08-02) — branch fix-reactor-builtins-undefined-functions

Added =#:reactor-type-name= and =#:document-reactor-registry= to the
reactor :IMPORT-FROM block in
=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/package.lisp=, so both now
resolve to the bound clautolisp.autolisp-runtime functions instead of
fresh unbound internal symbols. Regression test
=reactor-builtins-import-runtime-helpers= (builtin-tests.lisp) asserts
both names are present and fboundp in the builtins-core package —
failing before the import, passing after. builtins-core suite green
(1577/1577, incl. the new checks); no more "undefined function"
warnings for the two names in a from-scratch compile. Version 1.7.6 ->
1.7.7.

Follow-up filed (same bug class, out of scope here): the from-scratch
compile still warns =undefined variable *VLE-VECTOR-TOLERANCE*= in
=builtin-vle-g-vectol= — see
issues/open/vle-vector-tolerance-undefined-variable.issue.
